\section{Executive Summary (Option 1)}
% ============================================================

\paragraph{The stance (Option 1).}
At the most fundamental ``Level-0'' (the discrete substrate itself), the universal signal speed $\C$ is treated as a \emph{fundamental material constant} of the substrate. We do \emph{not} attempt to compute the SI number \SI{299792458}{m/s} from geometry alone; we instead show (without calibration/tuning) that:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item $\C$ is \emph{scale-invariant}: the lattice size $\aL$ cancels out \emph{exactly} under physically motivated scaling laws,
\item $\C$ is \emph{structure-fixed} at Point-J (jamming threshold): the dimensionless geometric factor becomes \emph{unity} without artificial parameter-fitting,
\item lattice dispersion becomes \emph{asymptotically perfectly linear} at long wavelength, with corrections $\mathcal{O}((\aL/\lambda)^2)$ and an \emph{analytic} prefactor (verified numerically).
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{What is ``no-tuning'' here?}
No step is allowed to multiply a theory curve by an arbitrary scaling factor to match data; no ratio (e.g.\ $k_2/k_1$) is chosen \emph{after} seeing the desired answer. The only tolerances appearing below are numerical convergence thresholds (floating-point noise), and unit normalizations ($\aL=1,\,m=1,\,k=1$) used to run toy simulations.

\paragraph{Core final formula.}
With the scaling rules
\[
m(\aL)=\rhozero\,\aL^3,\qquad F(\aL)=\Szero\,\aL^2,\qquad k(\aL)=\frac{F(\aL)}{\aL}=\Szero\,\aL,
\]
and with Point-J structural factor $\GammaS_J=1$, we obtain
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\;\C^2=\frac{\Szero}{\rhozero}\;}\qquad\text{(scale-invariant, no-tuning, no Planck numerics).}
\end{equation}
The job of geometry/jamming is to enforce $\GammaS_J=1$ (rather than forcing any ad-hoc $\phi$-tuning).

