\section{Static Speed Definition (No Clock)}
% ============================================================

\subsection{The static formula}
We fix the fundamental measurement definition:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\;\C^2=\frac{B_{\mathrm{eff}}}{\rho_{\mathrm{eff}}}\;}
\end{equation}
where $B_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is an effective stiffness modulus (in the \emph{relevant} continuum description) and $\rho_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is an effective inertial density.

\subsection{Important correction: $B_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is a \emph{derivative} stiffness (not max force/area)}
The earlier heuristic step
\[
B_{\mathrm{eff}} \stackrel{?}{\sim} \frac{F_{\max}}{\aL^2}
\]
has a conceptual hole: \textbf{bulk modulus (or stiffness modulus)} is defined by a small-strain derivative:
\[
B \equiv -V\frac{dP}{dV} \quad\text{(equivalently from the quadratic energy density in a small volumetric strain).}
\]
So voids/packing fraction generally affect both (i) mass density and (ii) stiffness, and must \emph{not} be inserted only into $\rho_{\mathrm{eff}}$.

