\section{Where the Old $\alpha/\sqrt{\phi}$ Logic Came From (and Why It Was a Hole)}
% ============================================================

\subsection{Old heuristic (historical record)}
The earlier attempt (kept here for completeness) was:
\[
\C = \sqrt{\frac{B_{\mathrm{eff}}}{\rho_{\mathrm{eff}}}},\qquad
B_{\mathrm{eff}} \approx \alpha_{\mathrm{geom}}^2\frac{F}{\aL^2},\qquad
\rho_{\mathrm{eff}}\approx \phiP\frac{m}{\aL^3}.
\]
Then
\[
\C \sim \sqrt{\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm{geom}}^2(F/\aL^2)}{\phiP(m/\aL^3)}}
= \alpha_{\mathrm{geom}}\frac{1}{\sqrt{\phiP}}\sqrt{\frac{F\aL}{m}}.
\]
If $\sqrt{F\aL/m}$ is identified with a target speed, the remaining factor is
\[
\C_{\mathrm{result}} = \C \left(\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm{geom}}}{\sqrt{\phiP}}\right).
\]
One then noted numerically:
\[
\alpha_{\mathrm{geom}}\approx 0.816,\quad
\frac{1}{\sqrt{\phiP}}\approx
\begin{cases}
1.25 & (\phiP\approx 0.64\ \mathrm{RCP})\\
1.16 & (\phiP\approx 0.74\ \mathrm{FCC})
\end{cases}
\Rightarrow
\alpha_{\mathrm{geom}}/\sqrt{\phiP}\approx 1.
\]

\subsection{Why that ``boost'' was artificial}
The hole is that $B_{\mathrm{eff}}$ was \emph{not} computed as a derivative stiffness. Once $B$ is computed correctly from a micro-model (Section~\ref{sec:microB}), the dependence on number density (hence $\phiP$) typically appears in $B$ as well, and \textbf{cancels} in $B/\rho$. Therefore the claimed $1/\sqrt{\phiP}$ speed-up is an artifact of an incorrect modulus definition.

% ============================================================
\section{Correct Micro--Macro Stiffness: Contact Network Derivations}
\label{sec:microB}
% ============================================================

We present two related micro-model routes, because we used \emph{two} kinds of lattice physics in the discussion:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item \textbf{Elastic/bulk route} (vector-like strain energy): corrects the $\phi$-hole and shows how $\phi$ cancels from $B/\rho$ when $Z,k,L$ are held fixed.
\item \textbf{Scalar graph-Laplacian route} (signal field on lattice): matches the FCC dispersion code exactly and gives a clean $\GammaS=\Z/6$ structure factor, becoming $\GammaS_J=1$ at Point-J where $\Z\simeq 6$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{(A) Elastic/bulk route (definition-level bulk modulus)}
Assume a contact network where each contact acts like a spring with stiffness $k$ and rest length $L$.

\paragraph{Number density.}
If grains of diameter $\aL$ occupy packing fraction $\phiP$, the number density scales as
\[
n \propto \frac{\phiP}{\aL^3}.
\]
(Exact prefactors depend on the grain volume model; they cancel below.)

\paragraph{Bond energy under isotropic strain.}
For a small isotropic strain $\varepsilon$, bond length changes $L\to L(1+\varepsilon)$, thus $\Delta L=L\varepsilon$.
A bond stores
\[
E_{\mathrm{bond}}=\frac12 k(\Delta L)^2=\frac12 k L^2\varepsilon^2.
\]
Bonds per volume: $(n\Z/2)$. Hence energy density:
\[
u=\frac{n\Z}{2}\cdot \frac12 kL^2\varepsilon^2=\frac{n\Z}{4}kL^2\varepsilon^2.
\]
By continuum definition, volumetric strain is $\Delta V/V=3\varepsilon$, and
\[
u=\frac12 B(3\varepsilon)^2=\frac{9}{2}B\varepsilon^2.
\]
Equating yields
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\;B=\frac{n\Z}{18}kL^2\;}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Density and speed.}
Mass density is $\rho=nm$. Therefore
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\;\C^2=\frac{B}{\rho}=\frac{\Z}{18}\frac{kL^2}{m}\;}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Key consequence.}
Since both $B$ and $\rho$ are proportional to $n$, the packing fraction dependence cancels in $B/\rho$ \emph{provided} $Z,k,L$ are held fixed:
\[
n\propto \phiP \quad\Rightarrow\quad \frac{B}{\rho}\propto \frac{n}{n}=\text{(independent of }\phiP\text{)}.
\]
Thus the earlier $1/\sqrt{\phiP}$ ``speed-up'' was not a valid inference from the definition of $B$.

\subsection{(B) Scalar graph-Laplacian route (matches FCC dispersion code)}
Now consider a scalar lattice field $u(\mathbf{x})$ that propagates on the neighbor graph.

\paragraph{Discrete dynamics.}
On each site $i$,
\begin{equation}
m\,\ddot u_i \;=\; k \sum_{j\in N(i)}(u_j-u_i).
\label{eq:scalar-dyn}
\end{equation}
This is the simplest local propagation law: ``each node is pulled toward neighbors''.

\paragraph{Plane-wave dispersion.}
For $u_i\propto e^{i(\Kvec\cdot \mathbf{x}_i-\omega t)}$,
\[
\omega^2(\Kvec)=\frac{k}{m}\sum_{\evec\in\mathcal{N}} 2\sin^2\!\Big(\frac{\Kvec\cdot\evec}{2}\Big).
\]
For small $|\Kvec|$:
\[
2\sin^2(\theta/2)=\frac{\theta^2}{2}+O(\theta^4),
\]
so
\[
\omega^2(\Kvec)\approx \frac{k}{m}\cdot \frac12\sum_{\evec}(\Kvec\cdot\evec)^2.
\]

\paragraph{Isotropic neighbor statistics.}
If neighbor directions are isotropic and $|\evec|=\aL$ for each neighbor, then
\[
\sum_{n=1}^{\Z}\hat u_n\hat u_n^{\mathsf T}\approx \frac{\Z}{3}\I,\qquad \evec_n=\aL\hat u_n
\]
hence
\[
\frac12\sum_{\evec}\evec\evec^{\mathsf T}\approx \frac12\cdot \aL^2\cdot \frac{\Z}{3}\I=\frac{\Z\aL^2}{6}\I.
\]
Therefore the long-wavelength speed is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\;\C^2 = \GammaS \,\frac{k}{m}\,\aL^2,\qquad \GammaS \equiv \frac{\Z}{6}\;}
\label{eq:GammaZ6}
\end{equation}
This $\GammaS$ is a \textbf{pure geometry/connectivity} factor.

\paragraph{Point-J closure (no tuning).}
At jamming (frictionless, 3D), the isostatic constraint count gives
\[
\Z_J\simeq 2d = 6,
\]
hence
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\;\GammaS_J = \frac{\Z_J}{6}=1\;}
\label{eq:Gamma1}
\end{equation}
Thus at Point-J the structure factor becomes \emph{exactly unity} by geometry/constraint counting, not by tuning.

\paragraph{Interpretation relative to the $B/\rho$ frame.}
For the scalar field, an effective continuum energy has the form
\[
U \sim \frac{B_{\mathrm{eff}}}{2}\int |\nabla u|^2\,dV,\qquad
T \sim \frac{\rho_{\mathrm{eff}}}{2}\int \dot u^2\,dV,
\]
whose Euler--Lagrange equation yields $\rho_{\mathrm{eff}}\ddot u = B_{\mathrm{eff}}\nabla^2 u$ and $\C^2=B_{\mathrm{eff}}/\rho_{\mathrm{eff}}$.
In this mapping, $B_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is a \emph{stiffness modulus for the field} (dimension of stress), not necessarily the mechanical bulk modulus of a granular solid. The FCC dispersion experiment below pertains to this scalar Laplacian model.

